---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import BrandMark from '../components/BrandMark.astro';
---
<Base title="About The Heraldry Project" description="The mission and methods of an independent heraldry academy created by James P. Howard, II.">
  <PageIntro eyebrow="Why this project exists" title="Why build this?" description="Heraldry becomes easier to understand when you can change a drawing, read its description, and follow a claim back to the document that supports it."/>
  <section class="section"><div class="wrap"><div class="notice-box" style="margin-bottom:28px"><strong>Publication and review.</strong><p>This independent teaching and research site contains original explanatory drafts undergoing independent specialist review. Factual corrections and limitations are recorded in the <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>; institutional primary records remain decisive for individual entitlement claims.</p></div></div>
    <div class="wrap split" style="align-items:start">
      <div><p class="eyebrow"><span class="star">✦</span> The premise</p><h2 class="section-title">Give the reader<br/>something to work with.</h2></div>
      <div style="font-size:1.05rem;line-height:1.9;color:var(--ink-2)">
        <p>Start with a shield on an old building. We can describe the drawing, but that leaves several questions: who displayed it, when it was made, whether it changed, and what the people using it meant to claim. Answering those questions takes us from art into history, genealogy, language, and the institutions that regulate arms. Occasionally it also requires an argument about a bird that doesn't have feet. The subjects belong together because the object makes us use them together.</p>
        <p>The Heraldry Project gives you a place to work through those connections. The readings establish the terms and methods; the drawing tools make changes visible; the object studies put the method to work on surviving evidence. The teaching shields are original examples, so you can experiment with them without confusing an exercise with somebody else's documented arms.</p>
        <p>The useful result is a reader who can explain what a shield shows and why a particular historical claim is justified. You should be able to identify the source, recognize the limits of what it establishes, and say what further evidence would help. Knowing the vocabulary makes that work easier; memorizing the vocabulary alone does not finish it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section-toned">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="rule-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span class="star">✦</span> The operating principles</p><h2 class="section-title">How the teaching works.</h2></div><p>Think of this as the project charter: the things that should still be true when we have a hundred more lessons and an absurd number of toys.</p></div>
      <div class="field-grid">
        <article class="field-panel"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="star">01</span> Intellectual seriousness</p><h3>Sources before stories.</h3><p>Claims about descent, jurisdiction, historical use and authorisation need evidence. A surname is useful for finding records; the records must establish the relationship.</p></article>
        <article class="field-panel"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="star">02</span> Learning by doing</p><h3>Manipulate the rules.</h3><p>A student should be able to turn a fess into a pale, change its tincture and see the blazon respond. That change makes a relationship visible which a definition alone may leave unclear.</p></article>
        <article class="field-panel"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="star">03</span> Access by design</p><h3>Ready wherever you are.</h3><p>Reading and practice should work well on a phone, with a keyboard, and with assistive technologies.</p></article>
        <article class="field-panel"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="star">04</span> Honest limitations</p><h3>A tool is not an authority.</h3><p>Our drawings are teaching studies, not certified unique arms. A simple pattern may coincide with historical armorial bearings. Automated blazons are limited. Legal recognition and armorial ownership need their own processes.</p></article>
        <article class="field-panel"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="star">05</span> Respect for history</p><h3>Identify the place and period.</h3><p>Traditions differ across regions and centuries. We make those distinctions explicit and preserve uncertainty when the record requires it.</p></article>
        <article class="field-panel"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="star">06</span> A living classroom</p><h3>Keep improving.</h3><p>Lessons, interactions, diagrams, sources and the artistic engine should develop together, with correction rather than false certainty.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="about-bio">
        <BrandMark />
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-on-dark"><span class="star">✦</span> The person responsible</p>
          <h2>James P. Howard, II</h2>
          <p>The Heraldry Project is part of a broader independent effort to make research, teaching, and serious scholarship more usable to the public. It is not sponsored, certified, or endorsed by any heraldic authority.</p>
          <a href="https://jameshoward.us/" rel="author">Visit jameshoward.us ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="split" style="padding-top:50px;gap:35px">
        <div><h2 class="small-title">What the browser remembers.</h2><p class="section-subtitle">Quiz completion and personal practice records are kept locally in your browser. Changing devices or clearing site storage may erase them. We do not send those scores to a server. Optional Analytics measures site traffic only after you accept. <a href="/privacy-and-cookie-notice/">Read the privacy and cookie notice</a>.</p></div>
        <div><h2 class="small-title">Who can answer an entitlement question.</h2><p class="section-subtitle">This is an educational site, not a grant-issuing body, accredited university, genealogical certification agency or source of legal advice. The College of Arms, the Court of the Lord Lyon, and the Canadian Heraldic Authority speak for themselves, not for us.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section-dark">
    <div class="wrap split" style="align-items:center">
      <div><p class="eyebrow eyebrow-on-dark"><span class="star">✦</span> Begin with an example</p><h2 class="section-title">Now, go make<br/>something.</h2></div>
      <div><p class="section-subtitle">Begin with a simple shield and learn which changes alter its identity. Quarterings will give us plenty to argue about once we can read the field.</p><a href="/academy/" class="btn btn-gold">Begin with lesson one ↗</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
